    "current_example": {
      "math_academy_id": "e-21727",
      "problem": "Consider the Lotka-Volterra model above, where $x=x(t)$ represents the predators and $y=y(t)$ represents the prey.\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=-0.4x+0.4xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=y-4xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluate the Jacobian of the system at the equilibrium point $\\mathbf{x^\\ast}=[0.25, \\, 1]^T,$ and classify this equilibrium point.",
      "worked_solution": "Let's denote $f_1(x,y)=-0.4x+0.4xy$ and $f_2(x,y)=y-4xy.$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-0.4x+0.4xy)=-0.4+0.4y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-0.4x+0.4xy)=0.4x \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y-4xy)=-4y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y-4xy)=1-4x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the Jacobian matrix is\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14149681/assets/a3784dd822ea14cbc29ce99ed7b9335c4c4316ba313d339de94b89e8b5ee1f95.svg)\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14149681/assets/d7cb25facfc5c8bff0b17a39e8d73b122c4974f020c3adb080903d367e277355.svg) to get\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14149681/assets/4fabaca9db5494cd7a500e7f4cc3304383fbff17e200bed6042aad47d7a63590.svg)\n\nTo find the eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix, we write down the characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}-\\lambda  & 0.1 \\\\ -4 & -\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-\\lambda )(-\\lambda )-(-4)\\cdot 0.1 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+0.4 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the equation, we get the eigenvalues $\\lambda_{1,2} = \\pm 0.2\\sqrt{10}\\,\\text{i}.$ Since the eigenvalues are purely imaginary, our equilibrium is a stable center.\n\n**Note:**\nFor the Lotka-Volterra model (as in this problem), it is safe to classify the nonzero equilibrium as a center when the linearization has purely imaginary eigenvalues. \n\n**Watch out!** \nIn contrast, for a general nonlinear system, the Jacobian test alone is inconclusive (additional analysis is required to determine whether the equilibrium is a true center or a spiral sink/source).",
      "html_path": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14149681/observations/step-e21727-8adb0cbe8d9e5d7f093d.html",
